    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "hey my electric water heater is making this really loud knocking sound every now and then and the water isnt getting that hot anymore. only lukewarm. what could be causing this?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The knocking sound your water heater is making is likely \"water hammer,\" which happens when sediment builds up at the bottom of the tank, acting like a piston that gets hit by rushing water. This sediment buildup is a classic sign that the water heater's anode rod has been consumed by the corrosive elements in your water supply, leaving the tank vulnerable to rust and scale.\n\nSince the water is only lukewarm now, the sediment layer is thick enough to insulate the heating elements or the burner from the water, effectively acting as a thermal barrier. It's a very inefficient setup, much like trying to heat a room with a heater covered in a thick blanket of insulation.\n\nYou should schedule a service call immediately to have a professional drain the tank and flush out the sludge. If you try to fix this yourself without proper tools, you risk damaging the unit further or creating a safety hazard with the hot water and electricity. It's better to let a certified technician handle the maintenance, especially since they know how to check the pressure relief valve and ensure the system is operating within safe limits.\n\nIn the meantime, try to avoid using the hot water excessively until the issue is resolved, as the system is already struggling to perform its function",
